# CMake out-of-tree build dirs (FetchContent deps land here)
**/cmake/build/
**/build/

# The rest of cmake/ is generated too, and both files say so in their own
# header ("AUTO-GENERATED by `tur fetch`. Do not edit."). `tur new` scaffolds
# exactly these three ignores into every new spice, and six spices here
# (plot, tls, tourist-ws, ws-client, ws-core, ws-server) already carry them in
# their own .gitignore -- the other 23 simply never got the rule, which is the
# whole of why the repo tracked some and not others.
#
# Committing them is not neutral. `tur test` / `tur build` read
# spice-deps-manifest.json "if present" and pass its include_dirs / link_dirs
# straight onto the compiler and linker command line. CMake writes that file
# at generate time, so a fetch whose configure FAILS leaves whatever was
# committed sitting there to be used -- and four of the committed manifests
# held absolute /Users/rjungemann/... paths, so a build on any other machine
# silently compiled against a directory that does not exist instead of
# cleanly having no dep flags.
**/cmake/CMakeLists.txt
**/cmake/spice-deps-manifest.json

/vendor/

# A spice's compiled image for `tur repl` and for notebook cells that import
# it (libturi's turi_env_attach_spice): built on demand, stamped with the tur
# that built it. `tur repl` also appends this to a spice's own .gitignore
# when it first creates the cache.
**/.tur-repl-cache/

# Object / archive artifacts
*.o
*.a
*.so
*.so.manifest
*.dylib

# macOS / editor noise
.DS_Store
*.swp

# Node
node_modules/

# Built site (regenerated by deploy-docs workflow)
web/dist/

# Generated docs HTML (source markdown stays in docs/guides/ and per-spice
# docstrings; regenerated by deploy-docs workflow via tools/genspices.py)
docs/html/

# Python bytecode cache from tools/
tools/__pycache__/

# `tur fetch` populates <spice>/spices/<dep>-<ref>/ with cloned dep
# trees (and, under the WT7 watch-cutover workaround, a symlink to the
# enclosing repo). These are package-manager output, not source.
spices/*/spices/

# Stray `tur emit-c` / `tur build` output that lands in cwd when invoked
# from the repo root. Real per-module emission goes under build/ or a target
# dir. Per-spice generated files are excluded via spices/*/.gitignore.
/event.c
/event.h
/*__*.c
/*__*.h
/*.manifest

# tur.lock is generated cache state, not a reproducibility manifest -- despite
# the "Commit this file to version control" header tur writes into it. Nothing
# in the toolchain checks out a locked SHA:
#
#   * `tur fetch` (no --update) treats the presence of a lock ROW as "already
#     on disk" and SKIPS the fetch entirely -- it never consults :resolved. On
#     a cold clone the dep is then silently absent, so a committed lock makes a
#     fresh checkout WORSE than no lock at all. Verified against spices/regex.
#   * The actual clone is `pkg_git_fetch(url, ref, dest)` -- by :ref, never by
#     :resolved -- so the recorded SHA pins nothing.
#   * `pkg_cmake_verify_lock` only compares deps already fetched into
#     cmake/build/_deps, so it is a local tamper-check and a no-op on a cold
#     clone.
#   * CI runs `tur fetch --update`, which bypasses both paths regardless.
#
# The files also carry a :fetched-at wall-clock stamp, so every developer's
# fetch rewrites them. The repo used to track 11 of 33 with no rule behind the
# split; they are all ignored now. If tur is later taught to check out
# :resolved, this decision is worth revisiting -- at that point committing them
# would start to mean something.
tur.lock
